    imageUrl: "https://www.trivianedge.com/og-image.png",
    faqs: [
      { question: "How much does a bad hire cost?", answer: "A widely quoted figure is about 30% of the employee’s first-year salary, although its original source is unclear. The true cost is usually higher because it also includes lost productivity, extra work for colleagues, delayed projects, lower morale, and the cost of hiring and onboarding a replacement." },
      { question: "What are the hidden costs of a bad hire?", answer: "They include the time senior colleagues spend reviewing and fixing work, delayed decisions and launches, lower morale and more resignations among strong performers, management time spent on performance issues, and the full cost of hiring again." },
      { question: "Why are bad hires harder to spot in remote teams?", answer: "Remote work removes the informal signals of an office, so problems appear only in output, which lags. Time-zone differences also slow down every correction." },
      { question: "How can you reduce the risk of a bad hire?", answer: "Assess candidates against real work scenarios, set clear goals for the first 30 days and review them honestly, make one person close to the team responsible for early check-ins, and deal with mismatches quickly." },
    ],
